# Line endings for the files a person double-clicks.
#
# Windows batch files and PowerShell scripts are stored WITH CRLF line endings
# and never converted ("-text"), so a downloaded ZIP, a git clone with any
# autocrlf setting, and a CI checkout all hand Windows the bytes it needs. A bare
# LF in a .bat can make cmd.exe mis-parse labels and multi-line blocks.
#
# Shell scripts, macOS .command files and .desktop entries are stored with LF
# only. A CRLF there breaks the "#!/bin/bash" line ("bad interpreter").
#
# Edit these files with an editor that keeps their line endings (or run
# "git add --renormalize ." and check "git ls-files --eol"). A test in
# pc/tests/test_installers.py fails when a .bat has a bare LF.
*.bat   -text
*.cmd   -text
*.ps1   -text
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.command   text eol=lf
*.desktop   text eol=lf
*.ico   binary
